UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p1", "partName": "Arya Samaj", "bodyMd": "**Background:** Arya Samaj gained strength in the Hyderabad state only after the movement for religious conversion into Islam had already begun. The organisation itself originated in North India and was especially powerful in Punjab, and certain peculiar local circumstances within the princely state helped it take root here too. In Arya Samaj''s own terminology, its followers described themselves not as \"Hindus\" but as \"Aryas.\"\n\n*   In **1875**, **Swamy Dayananda Saraswati** founded Arya Samaj in Bombay for the protection of Vedic dharma/culture. It is known as the **\"Vedic Dharma reform movement.\"**\n*   Dayananda gave the slogan **\"Back to Vedas.\"** His book **Satyarth Prakash** (\"Satyarth Prakashika\") was treated as a sacred text of the Samaj; in it he criticized Islam, Christianity, and Sikhism.\n*   The book was translated into Telugu by **Aadipudi Somanatha Rao**; its first Telugu edition was published in **1921**.\n*   Dayananda instructed all Arya Samaj members to perform **\"Sandhya Vandanam.\"** The Samaj''s principles were publicized through the newspaper **\"Vaidika Adarsh.\"**\n\n**Principles of Arya Samaj**\n\n*   No person is to be ascribed a caste by birth — people are categorized as Brahmin, Kshatriya, or Vaishya based on occupation.\n*   The Vedas are held to be eternal, infallible truths; the Samaj opposed priesthood, religious rites, and idol worship, while showing keen interest in Western scientific knowledge.\n*   It opposed the caste system, untouchability, and child marriage.\n*   It encouraged inter-caste marriage and widow remarriage.\n*   Followers sought to make Hindu religion accessible to all castes by eliminating idol worship and polytheistic ritual, reducing unnecessary orthodoxy, and removing untouchability.\n\n### Hyderabad Branch and Leadership\n\n*   The Hyderabad Arya Samaj branch was established in **1892** at Residency Bazar (Sultan Bazar) by **Swamy Nityananda**.\n\n| Position | Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1st President | Pandit Kamal Prasadji Mishra |\n| Secretary | Lakshman Desji |\n\n*   Important members included Swamy Shraddhanand, Pandit Narendraji, Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao, Keshav Rao Karotkar, Aadipudi Somanadha Rao, Aghoranath Chattopadhyaya, Narayan Rao Pawar, Vaman Rao Nayak, Damodara Satyalekar, Pandit Narendra, and Shyam Lal.\n*   **Pandit Keshav Rao Koratkar** was a leading public figure of the early 20th century, a great patriot and a prominent judge who served on the Hyderabad High Court under the Nizam''s government. He headed the state Arya Samaj until his death.\n\n### Conflict with Religious Conversion Movements\n\n*   During the Nizam period, an organisation called **\"Tabligh Islam\"** was established to convert non-Muslims to Islam.\n*   **Siddiqui Deendhar** preached that all Hindus should convert to Islam, claiming to be an incarnation of the god Chenna Basaveswara Swamy and asserting that the modern form of the Lingayat tradition was Islam; he denigrated Rama and Krishna in the process, and authored a book titled **\"Survere Alam.\"** Arya Samaj propagandists **Mangalorao Dev** and **Pandit Ramachandra Dahelvi** toured the state opposing his preaching.\n*   In **1930**, Arya Samaj resolved to firmly counter internal and external attacks on Hindu society, organizing programs to popularize Satyarth Prakash, havan-kund rituals, devotional hymns, and processions.\n*   **M.I.M. president Bahadur Yar Jung** attempted to convert Dalits to Islam through secret circulars; these were exposed to Arya Samaj by a postal employee, **Venkat Swamy**.\n\n### The Shuddhi Movement\n\n*   Dayananda Saraswati started the **\"Shuddhi Program\"** to re-convert Hindus who had converted to other religions.\n*   In Hyderabad, the program was led by **Swamy Shraddhanand**, for which he was murdered by **Khaja Hasan Nizam**.\n\n### Nizam Government''s Repression (1894–1936)\n\n*   In **1894**, the Nizam government expelled **Balakrishna Sharma** and **Nityananda** from the state for organizing Shuddhi programs.\n*   The government arrested Arya Samaj leaders **Vishvananda Saraswati** and **Vidhyananda Brahmachary**.\n*   Pandit Keshavrao proposed a resolution in **1930** in the Hyderabad state legislature to revive Hindu widow remarriage; conservative Hindu and Muslim members alike united to oppose it, but it was eventually enacted into law through the persistent efforts of **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**.\n*   Keshavrao died on **21 May 1932**; his death was an irreparable loss to Arya Samaj. His son, **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**, took over leadership and continued its activities with dedication, which increasingly troubled the Nizam government.\n*   In **1934**, the government banned the Arya Samaj newspaper **Vaidika Adarsh**, and separately barred central Arya Samaj propagandists **Pandit Ramachandra Dahelvi** and **Chandrabhanu** from touring the state. The same year, it made prior government permission compulsory for performing homas and havans in the provinces.\n*   In response, the central Arya Pratinidhi Sabha protested in 1934, claiming no formal ban on propagation existed. A delegation of **Narayanaswami, Swami Swatantranand, and Acharya Randevu** toured the state; although no explicit restriction was placed on this tour, conditions afterward reverted to their earlier adverse state.\n*   In **1935**, the Beedar (Bidar) district collector attacked Arya Samaj representatives at **Nilanga**, destroying havan vessels and temples. Arya Samaj protested strongly; the then Home Department secretary, **Zaheeruddin**, who investigated the matter, found fault with the collector''s conduct and recommended he personally bear the cost of rebuilding the temples and havan-kunds.\n\n### Arya Samaj Satyagraha (1938–39)\n\n*   On **24 October 1938**, the Hindu Civil Liberty Union and Arya Samaj conducted a **Satyagraha** against the Nizam government. **Pandit Narendraji** was arrested and sent to **Mannanuru jail** in Mahbubnagar district — called Telangana''s **\"Kalapani\"** (Black Water), situated near Amrabad in the Nallamalla forests.\n*   Narendraji had been associated with Arya Samaj since 1932; in 1938 he was arrested at Pandit Vinayakarao''s house during a religious ceremony and sent under ambush to Mannanuru, where he spent three years.\n*   After his release, Narendraji toured Warangal; **Kaloji Narayan Rao** wrote a poem in his honour — _\"Dairyam leni daddammalaku dairyamu cheppaga vachavu enadu\"_ (\"You came to instill courage in the fearful\").\n*   Narendraji''s remark that **\"Hyderabad state is a larger area jail\"** led the government to brand him a traitor and charge him with sedition, leading to re-arrest, despite numerous petitions from the central Arya Samaj body.\n*   A 17-year-old, **Ramachandra Rao**, continued shouting \"Vandemataram\" while being beaten by police, earning him the name **Vandemataram Ramachandra Rao**.\n*   Negotiations between Arya Samaj representative **Lala Deshbandhu Gupta** and the Nizam government led to the Satyagraha being withdrawn on **7 August 1939**.\n\n**All India Arya Samaj Conference, 1938**\n\n| Detail | Name |\n|---|---|\n| Held at | Sholapur, December 1938 |\n| President | Sri Madhava Rao Anne |\n| Chief Guest | Veer Savarkar |\n\n*   The conference discussed the \"Hyderabad problem\" and resolved to observe **20 January** as **\"Hyderabad Liberation Day\"** nationwide, sparking countrywide agitation.\n*   On **31 January 1939**, **Mahatma Narayanaswami**, attempting Satyagraha, was arrested as he entered the Hyderabad state border and externed at Sholapur. Twenty volunteers then continued Satyagraha in successive batches (_jattu_) at Gulbarga: the 2nd batch led by Kunwar Prasad, 3rd by Kushal Raj, 4th by Rajguru and Dhirendranath Shastri — all arrested. Despite mounting restrictions, workers'' enthusiasm only grew; the 5th batch was led by Pandit Vedavrata, 6th by Mahoshaya Krishna, and 7th by Gyanendra. The 8th batch was to be led by Vinayakrao Vidyalankar, but with the government announcing plans for political reforms, Arya Samaj — along with other political parties — temporarily suspended its movement.\n*   Across the state''s broader liberation struggle, around **12,000 Arya Samaj workers** were arrested in various circumstances.\n\n### Local and Educational Contributions\n\n*   The **\"Sadhanagandi\" Gym** was established in Warangal by Arya Samaj leader **Bolugodu Achari**, and further developed by **Bathini Moghilaiah Goud**.\n*   **Bommagani Dharmabhiksham** contributed to building the Arya Samaj mandir in **Suryapet**, and led a campaign against animal sacrifice during Dussehra and the Lingamanthula Swamy fair in the Suryapet region.\n*   Arya Samaj organized Hyderabad''s **first Ganesh celebrations** in **1895** at Shalibanda, Chadarghat.\n*   In **July 1940**, it established the **Kesav Memorial Educational Institutions** at Hyderabad.\n*   In **1954**, it established **Upadeshik Educational Institutions** at Nalgonda.\n\n### G.O. No. 53 and Renewed Repression (1937–1941)\n\n*   **G.O. No. 53 (1937)** — the _\"Firman\"_ order curbing freedom of speech — came into force, requiring government permission to construct havan-kunds. Arya Samaj never accepted this order.\n*   Communal disturbances broke out at **Gulbarga** on a Holi festival day when colors were thrown on a Muslim individual. **Lal Singh, Hanumantha Rao**, and other leading Arya Samaj workers were arrested.\n*   Riots that broke out in **Dhoolpet** on **16 April** took a long time to subside; **21 Arya Samaj workers** were arrested in connection.\n*   On **22 January 1938**, **Dharma Prakash** was murdered at Kalyani.\n*   During Dasara festivities at **Udgir**, **Shyamlal** was arrested on the pretext of maintaining peace; he died in **Bidar jail on 17 December**, a death the government never investigated.\n*   In **1941**, Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar was elected head of the state Arya Samaj, with Narendraji as secretary. Though the organisation had temporarily suspended active agitation, government restrictions on it did not ease:\n    *   **''Om'' pendants** were banned in Damaragidda, Gulbarga district.\n    *   Workers **Gangaram, Ganapatirao, Dattatreya**, and others were murdered in Udgir, Nanded district.\n    *   The Arya Samaj temple at **Alland** was sealed on **4 June 1941**.\n    *   Fourteen workers were arrested; schools they ran at **Ahmedpur** and **Chakur** were shut; a havan school at Sakoli was never allowed to open. Some workers were subjected to severe punishment despite limited scope for wrongdoing.\n\n### Freedom Struggle (1942–1947)\n\n*   **1942–43** was an especially harsh period: workers lived in constant fear for their lives; local Urdu papers spread propaganda against Arya Samaj; the Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen''s campaign branding it a purely religious-fanatic body failed to convince the public — in reality, Arya Samaj workers fought shoulder-to-shoulder with the State Congress in the freedom struggle.\n*   At the **1942 Ughir Arya Pratinidhi Sabha**, Vinayakarao and Narendraji were re-elected chair and secretary; twelve resolutions were passed, chiefly opposing the government''s rushed implementation of constitutional reforms, and warning the government would bear responsibility for any adverse consequences.\n*   Hindu shops were looted in **Owrad, Bidar district**; a Hindu worker who protested was murdered — an incident that deeply pained Mahatma Gandhi, who sent Rs. 1,500 in relief to the affected.\n*   Arya Samaj itself split into two factions.\n*   On **3 March 1942**, communal rioting during an Arya procession at **Hanmakonda** killed **Shivachandra** and three of his followers.\n*   Similar communal disturbances occurred at **Nagarkurnool** and **Hingoli**, where the government one-sidedly restricted only the Hindu side, exposing its bias; comparable organised attacks occurred at **Jogipet** and **Tarafeed**.\n*   In **December 1942**, police at Gulbarga failed to prevent the looting of Hindu shops; that same year, communal clashes at **Nizamabad** went uninvestigated.\n*   By **1944**, an estimated **300 Arya Samaj branches** were active in the state, though exact participation figures in the freedom movement are unclear.\n*   At the **1945 Gulbarga annual Arya Samaj convention**, attending volunteers faced a police lathi-charge, in which **Pandit Narendraji** was severely injured.\n\n### Towards Integration (1947–1949)\n\n*   On **15 August 1947**, India gained independence, but the Nizam dreamed of separate sovereignty for Hyderabad state.\n*   Police arrested **Pandit Narendraji**, who was organizing to have Hyderabad State merge immediately with the Indian Union.\n*   On **2 September 1947**, at a national flag-hoisting meeting in **Parkal** attended by 1,500 people, police firing injured many.\n*   Between **August 1947 and September 1949**, severe unrest gripped the state; Razakar atrocities forced residents to take temporary shelter in camps at **Sholapur, Pandharpur, Barsi, Bijapur, Umarkhed, Balda, Amravati, and Vijayawada**. Arya Samaj gave them generous financial assistance.\n*   **Vinayak Kumar, Satyanarayan Singh, Madan Mohan**, and others gathered secret intelligence on Razakar activities and passed it to Pandit Vinayakarao; on discovering this, the government arrested him.\n*   The \"Vandemataram brothers,\" undeterred by the risks, continued secret exchanges and passed information about foreign arms being smuggled into the state to **India''s Agent-General, K.M. Munshi**.\n*   **Mahatma Anand Swami** sent Rs. 2,000 every month to patriots suffering under police repression.\n*   Arya Samaj maintained a central office in Hyderabad, with branches across several districts extending later to taluka centres. Some Muslim traders under government patronage opposed it, while Marwadi and north Indian settlers tended to support it; its strength within Telangana outside Hyderabad city remained comparatively limited.\n\n### Legacy\n\n*   Arya Samaj was fundamentally a religious organisation, and its members strongly opposed Gandhian non-violence in principle even as they took an active part in politics.\n*   **Pandit Vinayakarao Vidyalankar**, the leading Arya Samaj figure of the era, later became a minister in **Burgula Ramakrishna Rao''s** cabinet. With the fall of the Nizam''s government and the establishment of Congress rule, the organisation lost its distinct political standing.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p2", "partName": "Brahma Samaj and Theosophical Society", "bodyMd": "*   **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** founded Brahma Samaj in **1828** in Bombay.\n*   Its Hyderabad branch was established in **1869**.\n*   Hyderabad''s first Brahma Samaj meeting was held at S' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'ultan Bazar in **1914**, presided over by **Narayana Govind Vellinkar**.\n*   The important Andhra-region member of Brahma Samaj at the time was **Raghupati Venkataratnam Naidu**.\n\n### Theosophical Society\n\n*   Founded in **New York in 1875** by **Madam Helena Blavatsky** (Russia) and **Colonel Olcott** (America).\n*   Its Hyderabad branch was established in **1882** at Chaderghat.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p3", "partName": "Other Socio-Political Organizations", "bodyMd": "| Organisation | Founded | Founder(s)/Leaders | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hindu Social Club | 1892 | Raja Murali Manohar Bahadur | Opposed discrimination against Hindu students sent to England for higher education under the Nizam |\n| Society of Union & Progress | 1929 | Mir Akbar Ali Khan, Padmaja Naidu | Formed against religious indifference shown by the Nizam government |\n| Swadeshi League | 1930 | Padmaja Naidu (Secretary: Fazlu Rehaman) | Spread the importance of Khadi |\n| Nizam Subjects League (also \"Mulki League,\" 1935) | 1934 | President: Sir Nizamath Jung | For protection of Mulki and civil rights |\n| Hyderabad Social Service League | 1915 | Keshav Rao Koratkar, Vaman Nayak | With Ganapati Rao Hardikar, founded the Vivekavardini (Telugu-medium) school in 1906 |\n| Hyderabad Young Men''s Union | 1917 | President: Vaman Nayak | — |\n| Hyderabad State Reforms Association | 1918 | Keshav Rao Karotkar, Vaman Nayak | — |\n| Hindu Dharma Parishad | 1925 | Presided by Raja Pratap Giriji | Bhagya Reddy Varma introduced a proposal for Harijan development; special guest Pandit Sheshadri |\n| Hindu Standing Committee (Hindu Sthayi Sangam) | 1932 | Kashinath Rao Vaidya | Founded at Residency Bazar |\n| Humanitarian League | — | Rai Balamukund (with Bhagya Reddy Varma, Lalji Meghaji) | Fought for Harijan welfare and development |\n| Comrades Association | 13 Dec 1939 | Raja Bahadur Gour, Makhdoom Mohiuddin, Omkar Prasad, Syed Ibrahim | Inaugural president: Kashinath Rao Vaidya |\n", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p4", "partName": "Student Organizations", "bodyMd": "*   India''s first student organisation, the **All India Students Federation**, was founded in **1936** in Lucknow.\n*   Its subsidiary branch, the **All Hyderabad Student Union (AHSU)**, was founded in **1941** at Hyderabad.\n*   **Aquil Ali Khan** and **Omkar Prasad** played important roles in AHSU.\n*   **Anabheri Prabhakar Rao**, from Karimnagar, was among the students who joined AHSU.\n*   With AHSU''s establishment, student hostels turned into political centres and political discussions flourished there.\n*   Many student leaders active in AHSU later became members of the **Andhra Maha Sabha** and the **Communist Party**.\n*   AHSU also played an active role in the **\"Join India\"** campaign before Hyderabad''s merger with the Indian Union.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p5", "partName": "Women''s Organizations", "bodyMd": "| Organisation | Year | Founder(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bharatha Mahila Samajam | 1907 | Ravichettu Lakshmi Narasamma (with Sitabai) | First women''s organisation in Hyderabad; founded at Narasamma''s house |\n| Hyderabad Women''s Social Development Association | 1916 | Sarojini Naidu (with Margaret sisters, Amina Begum Hydari, Rustonji Fardhun) | Worked as an affiliate of the All India Women Federation |\n| Yuvati Sharanalayam | 1922 | Yamini Purnatilakam (Secunderabad) | Provided shelter/support for oppressed women, including prostitutes |\n| Andhra Sodari Samajam | 1925 | Nadimpalli Sundaramma, Pandita Veera Raghavamma, Lokanandi Janakamma, Puram Rukminamma, Chatrati Laxminarasamma | Published the magazine \"Savitri\" |\n| Andhra Mahila Sangam | 1930 | Durgabhai Deshmukh and others | — |\n| Andhra Yuvati Mandali | 1935 | Ellapragada Sitakumari, Illandula Saraswati Devi | Later developed by Burgula Ananta Laxmi, Obulreddy Rangamma; established a Shishuvihar in Hyderabad |\n| Arya Yuvajana School | 1938 | Sumitradevi (Chikkadpally) | — |\n| Navajeevan Mandali | 1937 | Chaired by Prameela Bhai | — |\n| All India Mahila Sabha (Hyderabad hostel) | 1931 | — | Opened a hostel for working women; set up rural medical units, education, adult education, training and employment programs |\n\n*   **Sangam Lakshmibai**, popularly known as **\"Telangana Jhansi Lakshmibai,\"** took part in the anti-Nizam struggle and was jailed.\n*   **Muslim Women''s Associations** included Anjuman-e-Kavatik-Deccan, Anjuman-e-Islam, Anjuman-e-Sirajul Kawatik, Lady Hyder Club, and Lady Burdans Club.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p6", "partName": "Labour Unions and Trade Associations", "bodyMd": "*   India''s first labour union, the **All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC)**, was founded by **N.M. Joshi**.\n*   Hyderabad''s first labour union, the **Calendar Company Workers Union**, was founded by **V.V. Giri** in Secunderabad in **1920**.\n*   In **1928**, railway employees went on strike for the first time in Hyderabad — the **first strike in Hyderabad''s history** — led by **Fatehullah Khan** and **Raghavendra Rao**.\n*   In **1935**, **Devuri Shesha Giri Rao** founded the **Singareni Collieries Workers Union**.\n*   The labour union leader of **Warangal Azam Jahi Mills** was **Sarvadevabhatla Ramanadham**.\n*   **Vattikota Alwar Swamy** became a popular labour union leader in Secunderabad during this period.\n*   In **1941**, the Railway Workers Union went on strike, supported by the **Comrades Association** under **Makhdoom Mohiuddin**.\n*   All the state''s labour unions eventually united to form the **All Hyderabad Trade Union Congress (AHTUC)** — President: Makhdoom Mohiuddin; Secretary: **Dr. Raj Bahadur Gour**.\n*   On **16 August 1946**, AHTUC merged with AITUC.\n\n### Trade (Merchant) Associations\n\n*   Under the Nizam, traders faced repeated exploitation: they were required to supply goods free of cost to visiting government officials, and at meagre prices to Deshmukhs, Deshpandes, Patels, and Patwaris.\n*   The **Suryapet Traders Association** was founded on **2 December 1922** by **Puvvada Venkatappaiah, Bhagari Laxmaiah**, and others; its rules and regulations, drafted by **Madapati Hanumanth Rao**, became a model for other trader associations.\n*   The **Bhuvanagiri Traders Association** (Bhuvanagiri Mitra Mandali) was formed with the support of **Arutla Laxmi Narsimha Reddy**; its secretary was **G.S. Gupta**.\n\n**Traders Mahasabhas**\n\n| Sabha | Year |\n|---|---|\n| Suryapet Sabha | 1923 |\n| Miryalagudem Sabha | 1928 |\n| Nalgonda Sabha | 1928 |\n| Tipparthi Sabha | 1929 |\n\n*   The president of the Tipparthi meetings was **Pulijala Venkat Ranga Rao**. At the Tipparthi meeting, **Pabbati Yellaiah** published a book, **\"Vartaka Swatantryam,\"** written by **Mandamula Ramachandra Rao** on behalf of the Tipparthi Traders Association.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana-p7", "partName": "Caste Associations", "bodyMd": "| Sangam | Founded/Year | Key Persons | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reddy Sangam | 1918 | Raja Bahadur Venkata Rama Reddy | Reddy hostel admitted students irrespective of caste or religion |\n| Yadava Sangam | 1920 | Seetaramaiah Yadav | — |\n| Mudiraj Sangam | 1922 | President: G. Ramakrishnaiah | Mudiraj Mahasabha held in 1922 |\n| Munnuru Kapu Sangam | 1922 (Mahasabha 1935) | — | 1935 Nizam Rashtra Andhra Munnuru Kapu Mahasabha; book \"Munnuru Kapu Kula Abyudayam\" authored by Bojja Narsimhulu |\n| Gouda Sangam | — | Chiragu Veeranna | Established hostels for Goud students |\n| Padmashali Sangam | — | Konda Laxman Bapuji, Guntaka Narsaiah Panthulu, Hakim Narayan Das | 1929 All India Padmashali Sabha held in Hyderabad; 1950 Hyderabad Handloom Weavers Central Co-operative Association founded (later renamed **HYCO**); book \"Mohturbha Maggam Pannu\" (criticizing the weaving tax) by Mateti Papayya; 1946 Nizam State Padmashali Mahasabha held in Warangal, presided over by Konda Laxman Bapuji |\n| Vaishya Sangam (Vaishya Yuva Jana Sangam) | 1939 | K.C. Gupta | — |\n| Vishwa Brahman Sabha | — | Chintapalli Raghava Chary | — |\n| Sri Vaishnava Mahasabha | — | Tirunagari Venkatappayya | — |\n| Kalavanthula Sangam | — | Tirunagari Venkatappayya | — |\n", "free": false}]' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter22-socio-cultural-revival-in-telangana';
